---
title: Login page branding
description: Making the hosted login page look like your product.
section: Configuring a tenant
---

GateKeeper hosts the login, sign-up, MFA, and password-reset screens for you. Hosting them
centrally is what lets the risk engine and MFA work at all — but it means the pages are *yours* to
brand. That is the **Appearance** tab on each OAuth client.

Appearance is per client, so two apps in the same tenant can look completely different.

## Layouts

| Layout | Looks like |
| --- | --- |
| `centered` | Form centered on a plain background. |
| `split_image` | Brand image on one side, form on the other. |
| `card` | Form in a floating card over a background image. |
| `minimal` | Compact — logo and form, nothing else. |

`split_image` additionally lets you put the hero image on the **left** or the **right**.

## The rest of the knobs

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| **Logo URI** | `http(s)` URL, rendered in an `<img>`. |
| **Background image URI** | `http(s)` URL. Used by `split_image` and `card`. |
| **Heading / subtext** | Rendered as plain text. |
| **Primary / accent color** | Validated hex. |
| **Border radius** | Applied to inputs and buttons. |
| **Font** | One of `inter`, `system`, `roboto`, `georgia`. |

## Why you cannot inject CSS

You may notice there is no "custom CSS" box, and that fonts are a fixed list of four rather than a
free-text field. That is intentional, and it is not an oversight to work around.

The login page is where users type their password. Anything that can inject styles or markup into
that page can also reposition it, overlay it, or restyle it into a convincing phishing surface —
and an administrator account is not the same trust level as the server itself. So every value here
is constrained by construction: layouts and fonts are closed sets, colors are validated hex, image
fields must be `http(s)` URLs, and all text is rendered as escaped text nodes.

> [!NOTE]
> The font names map to a bundled, safe font stack. A raw `font-family` string is never passed
> through.

If you need branding beyond what these fields allow, the honest answer is that you want your own
login UI driving the API directly — not a more permissive hosted page.
